    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5670/oceanog.2009.65",
    "abstract": "In only 10 years, the Argo Program has grown from an idea into a functioning global observing system for the subsurface ocean. More than 3000 Argo floats now cover the world ocean. With these instruments operating on 10-day cycles, the array provides 9000 temperature/salinity/depth profiles every month that are quickly available via the Global Telecommunications System and the Internet. Argo is recognized as a major advance for oceanography, and a success for Argo\u2019s parent programs, the Global Ocean Data Assimilation Experiment and Climate Variability and Predictability, and for the Global Earth Observation System of Systems. The value of Argo data in ocean data assimilation (ODA) and other applications is being demonstrated, and will grow as the data set is extended in time and as experience in using the data set leads to new applications. The spatial coverage and quality of the Argo data set are improving, with consideration being given to sampling under seasonal ice at higher latitudes, in additional marginal seas, and to greater depths. Argo data products of value in ODA modeling are under development, and Argo data are being tested to confirm their consistency with related satellite and in situ data. Maintenance of the Argo Program for the next decade and longer is needed for a broad range of climate and oceanographic research and for many operational applications in ocean state estimation and prediction.",
    "field": "Global ocean observation",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of sustaining and evolving the global Argo ocean observing system by improving incomplete spatial coverage, correcting systematic data errors, and expanding sampling capabilities to new depths and regions for improved ocean state estimation and climate research.",
    "method": "This paper describes the Argo Program, a global observing system of over 3000 profiling floats, as a method for obtaining continuous, high-quality subsurface ocean profiles to overcome the limitations of sparse historical data for climate and oceanographic research.",
    "task": "This paper's task is to review the first decade of the Argo global ocean observing system, assessing its progress and value in data assimilation, and outlining the challenges and future plans for maintaining and enhancing the array for climate research and operational oceanography.",
